Frontend Development Interview Preparation Guide

This guide introduces core HTML concepts that frequently come up in frontend interviews. Each answer includes enough context to explain not just what something is, but when and why to use it.

HTML interview questions and answers

1. What is HTML?

Answer: HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard markup language used to describe the structure and meaning of content on the web. Elements such as headings, paragraphs, links, and forms tell browsers what the content represents. CSS controls presentation, while JavaScript adds behavior.

2. What is the difference between an HTML element and a tag?

Answer: A tag is the markup written between angle brackets, such as `<p>` or `</p>`. An element is the complete structure, usually including its opening tag, content, and closing tag: `<p>Hello</p>`. Some elements, such as `<img>`, are void elements and do not have closing tags or contain child content.

3. What does `<!doctype html>` do?

Answer: It tells the browser to interpret the document using modern HTML standards mode. It is a declaration, not an HTML element, and should appear at the very start of an HTML document. Omitting it can make browsers use quirks mode, where some layout behavior imitates older browsers.

4. What is semantic HTML, and why does it matter?

Answer: Semantic HTML uses elements that describe the purpose of their content. For example, use `<nav>` for navigation, `<main>` for the page's main content, and `<button>` for an action instead of choosing elements only for their default appearance. Semantics help assistive technologies and search engines interpret a page, and make code easier to understand and maintain.

5. What is the difference between `<div>` and `<span>`?

Answer: Both are generic containers with no inherent semantic meaning. `<div>` is a block-level container by default and is commonly used to group larger sections; `<span>` is an inline container commonly used to target a small part of text. Prefer a meaningful element such as `<section>` or `<strong>` when it describes the content better.

6. What is the difference between block-level and inline elements?

Answer: In traditional CSS terms, block elements such as `<div>` normally start on a new line and take the available width, while inline elements such as `<span>` flow within a line of text. These are default display behaviors, not immutable HTML rules: CSS can change an element's `display`, and modern layout commonly uses flexbox and grid.

7. What are the main parts of a basic HTML document?

Answer: A typical document includes the doctype, an `<html>` root, a `<head>` for metadata and linked resources, and a `<body>` for visible page content. For example:

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>Example page</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <h1>Hello, web</h1>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
```

8. What is the purpose of the `<head>` element?

Answer: The `<head>` contains document metadata and references to resources; it is not the place for the page's main visible content. Common contents include the page `<title>`, character encoding, viewport settings, SEO metadata, stylesheets, and scripts.

9. Why should you set the page language with the `lang` attribute?

Answer: Setting a language on the root element, for example `<html lang="en">`, helps screen readers pronounce content correctly and helps browsers and search engines identify its language. Use the appropriate language tag for the page's primary language, and mark sections in another language when needed.

10. What are HTML attributes?

Answer: Attributes provide additional information or configuration for an element. They are written in an opening tag, usually as a name-value pair, such as `<a href="/about">About</a>`. Some attributes are boolean: their presence means true, as with `required`; their absence means false.

11. What is the difference between `id` and `class`?

Answer: An `id` identifies one element within a document and should be unique on that page. A `class` can be shared by any number of elements. Both can be used in CSS and JavaScript, but classes are generally preferable for reusable styling and behavior.

12. What is the difference between `<section>`, `<article>`, and `<main>`?

Answer: `<main>` identifies the document's primary content and should generally appear only once per page. `<section>` groups content around a common topic, often with a heading. `<article>` represents self-contained content that could stand on its own or be distributed independently, such as a news story or forum post.

13. How do you create an accessible image?

Answer: Add an `alt` attribute that conveys the image's purpose or useful information. If an image is purely decorative and adds no information, use `alt=""` so assistive technology can skip it. Avoid repeating nearby text in the alternative text, and do not use a filename as a substitute for a useful description.

```html
<img src="team.jpg" alt="The support team at the company office">
```

14. What is the difference between `<a>` and `<button>`?

Answer: Use an `<a>` element with an `href` to navigate to another location, such as a page, file, or section. Use a `<button>` to perform an action on the current page, such as submitting a form, opening a dialog, or toggling a setting. Choosing the correct element gives users and assistive technology the expected keyboard and interaction behavior.

15. How do HTML forms work?

Answer: A `<form>` groups controls whose values can be submitted to a server or handled by the page. Its `action` specifies the submission destination and its `method` specifies how data is sent. Each submitted control needs an appropriate `name`; labels should be associated with controls so they are understandable and easy to use.

```html
<form action="/subscribe" method="post">
  <label for="email">Email address</label>
  <input id="email" name="email" type="email" required>
  <button type="submit">Subscribe</button>
</form>
```

16. What is the difference between GET and POST in a form?

Answer: `GET` submits form data as part of the URL, making it suitable for retrieving or filtering information that can be bookmarked or shared. `POST` sends data in the request body and is typically used for actions that create or change data. POST does not itself encrypt data or make it secure; use HTTPS and validate and authorize requests on the server.

17. Why should form controls have labels?

Answer: A `<label>` gives a form control a readable name, helps screen-reader users understand its purpose, and provides a larger clickable target. Associate it by matching the label's `for` value to the control's `id`, or by nesting the control inside the label. Placeholder text is not a replacement for a label.

18. What are native HTML form validation features?

Answer: HTML provides built-in constraints such as `required`, input types like `email`, and limits such as `min`, `max`, `minlength`, and `pattern`. The browser can report when a value does not meet these constraints. This improves usability, but client-side validation can be bypassed, so the server must validate submitted data too.

19. What is the difference between `async` and `defer` on a script?

Answer: Both let an external script download without blocking HTML parsing. A `defer` script runs after parsing is complete and preserves the order of deferred scripts. An `async` script runs as soon as it has downloaded, so its execution order is not guaranteed. Use `defer` for scripts that depend on the parsed page or one another; use `async` for independent scripts.

20. What are `data-*` attributes?

Answer: `data-*` attributes let authors attach custom data to an HTML element, for example `<button data-product-id="42">Add to cart</button>`. JavaScript can read them through the element's `dataset` property, such as `button.dataset.productId`. They are useful for small pieces of element-specific data, but should not replace semantic HTML, accessible labels, or application state.

21. What are void elements in HTML?

Answer: Void elements cannot contain child content and do not have closing tags. Common examples include `<img>`, `<input>`, `<br>`, `<hr>`, `<meta>`, and `<link>`. In HTML, write them without a closing slash, for example `<img src="photo.jpg" alt="A mountain view">`.

22. How do you display reserved characters in HTML?

Answer: Use character references when a character would otherwise be interpreted as markup or when you need a specific symbol. For example, `&lt;` displays `<`, `&gt;` displays `>`, and `&amp;` displays `&`. Numeric references such as `&#169;` can also represent characters.

23. What is the difference between `<strong>` and `<b>`?

Answer: `<strong>` indicates that its content is important, serious, or urgent; browsers commonly render it in bold, and assistive technologies may convey its importance. `<b>` draws attention to text without adding that same importance. Choose based on meaning rather than appearance.

24. How do you make an HTML table accessible?

Answer: Use tables for tabular data, not for page layout. Add `<th>` elements for headers, associate headers with data cells using `scope` where appropriate, and provide a `<caption>` to describe the table's purpose. This helps users understand the relationship between rows and columns.

```html
<table>
  <caption>Interview schedule</caption>
  <thead>
    <tr><th scope="col">Name</th><th scope="col">Time</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Asha</th><td>10:00</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
```

25. What is the purpose of the `target` and `rel` attributes on a link?

Answer: The `target` attribute controls where a link opens. For example, `target="_blank"` opens it in a new browsing context. When opening a new tab, use `rel="noopener"` to prevent the opened page from accessing the original page through `window.opener`. Add `noreferrer` as well when the referrer should not be sent.

```html
<a href="https://example.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Visit example</a>
```

26. How do you provide responsive images in HTML?

Answer: Use `srcset` and `sizes` on `<img>` to let the browser choose an appropriate image file for the screen's resolution and layout size. Use `<picture>` with `<source>` elements when you need to offer different image formats or art direction, and include a fallback `<img>` with useful alternative text.

27. How do you add audio or video to a web page?

Answer: Use `<audio>` or `<video>` with the `controls` attribute to provide the browser's playback controls. Include one or more `<source>` elements with the media type, and provide fallback text for unsupported browsers. For video with spoken content, provide captions using a `<track>` element.

```html
<video controls>
  <source src="intro.mp4" type="video/mp4">
  <track src="captions-en.vtt" kind="captions" srclang="en" label="English">
  Your browser does not support video.
</video>
```

28. What is an `<iframe>`, and how can you use it more safely?

Answer: An `<iframe>` embeds another HTML page within the current page. Give it a descriptive `title` so users of assistive technology know what it contains. Use the `sandbox` attribute to restrict the embedded content's capabilities, granting only the permissions it needs. For example, `<iframe title="Map" src="/map" sandbox></iframe>` starts with sandbox restrictions enabled.

29. What is the difference between a submit button and a regular button in a form?

Answer: A `<button>` inside a form defaults to `type="submit"` and submits the form. Use `type="button"` for a button that performs another action without submitting, such as opening a menu. Explicitly setting the type helps avoid accidental form submissions.

30. What does the `loading="lazy"` attribute do?

Answer: Setting `loading="lazy"` on an `<img>` or `<iframe>` allows the browser to defer loading it until it is near the visible part of the page, which can reduce initial loading work. Avoid lazy-loading important above-the-fold images, since delaying them can make the page feel slower.

Interview practice tips

- Explain the reasoning behind your choice of element, not just its name.
- Give a small example, especially for semantics, accessibility, and forms.
- Distinguish HTML's meaning and structure from CSS presentation and JavaScript behavior.
- Mention that browser-side checks improve usability but do not replace server-side validation.
